Chapter: ConsoleReadlineCite
  cites Foreword chapter Console
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe

 Citing Console does not disable read-line.

 read-line is two things at once: a builtin with a real emitter, and an
 operation declared on the Console effect. Declaring it gives it a
 handler-table slot, and emit-name used to consult that slot before the
 builtin table. No handler ever writes the slot, so the read came back
 zero, and zero decodes as Just "" -- an empty line, indistinguishable
 from a real one. Any program that cited Console read an empty line from
 the terminal and carried on. Dropping the cite fixed it, which is the
 opposite of what a cite is for.

 A name with a real implementation now outranks an unwritten handler
 slot. Inside a handle block the operation is still bound as a local, so
 a handler that means to override read-line still does.

 With no stdin this must be None, the honest end of input. It was
 Just "" for as long as the slot won.

Section: Definitions

  render : Maybe Text -> Text
  render (m) =
   when m
    is Just (t) -> "read-line: Just [" & t & "]"
    is None -> "read-line: None"

  opening : [Console.Read, Console.Write] Nothing
  opening = act
    s <- read-line
    print-line-uni (render s)
  end

